# Watcher — environment configuration (placeholders)
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. The `<...>` values are the items tagged NEEDS INPUT
# in docs/build-spec-addendum.md §17. In production these are per-tenant and
# encrypted at rest (§3); this file is for local/dev and non-secret config.
#
# Every variable below is read by `apps/api/core/config.py` (Settings). A value
# still wrapped in angle brackets counts as unset: fields with a default fall
# back to it, and a subsystem that needs one it hasn't got fails at startup
# naming the variable, rather than sending "<ANTHROPIC_API_KEY>" to a provider.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Meta WhatsApp Business Cloud API — ingestion (§5) — NEEDS INPUT
# Source: Meta App dashboard → WhatsApp → API setup
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
META_APP_ID=<META_APP_ID>
META_APP_SECRET=<META_APP_SECRET>                    # verifies X-Hub-Signature-256 on every webhook POST (§5)
META_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=<CHOOSE_A_VERIFY_TOKEN>    # echoed back during the webhook subscription handshake
META_GRAPH_API_VERSION=<GRAPH_API_VERSION>           # e.g. v21.0 — pin the Graph API version
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=<WHATSAPP_PERMANENT_ACCESS_TOKEN>

# WhatsApp number identifiers — maps to tenants.waba_id / phone_number_id (§4) — NEEDS INPUT
WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID=<WABA_ID>
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=<PHONE_NUMBER_ID>           # Meta's numeric ID for the number, not the number itself
WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_NUMBER_E164=<+9665XXXXXXXX>        # the watched business number, E.164 format
CONTROL_CHAT_PHONE_E164=<+9665XXXXXXXX>              # operator number: control-chat pings (§10) AND emergency alerts (G3)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime vocabulary for this single-vertical deployment. Unset preserves the
# existing holiday-home behavior. The DermaClub API and worker both use clinics.
# Any value without a shipped vocabulary fails during startup.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TENANT_VERTICAL=holiday_homes

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the properties are (G3). An IANA zone name, used to read the one
# emergency trigger that only fires at night — locked out at 2pm is a support
# request, at 2am it is a person on a street. It is the guest's clock that
# decides that, not the container's. Defaults to Asia/Dubai; Africa/Cairo for
# an Egyptian client. A name this machine cannot resolve fails at startup.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TENANT_TIMEZONE=Asia/Dubai

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The number a customer may ring themselves in an emergency (G3)
# Distinct from CONTROL_CHAT_PHONE_E164, which is where the *alert* goes: this
# is what the customer is told. For a clinic the two are rarely the same — the
# alert wakes whoever is on call, while the number a patient rings for a filler
# occlusion is a named clinician. Unset, the emergency reply names no number.
#
# TENANT_EMERGENCY_REPLY replaces the default wording entirely and may contain
# {contact}. Set it for any tenant that must NOT direct customers to public
# emergency services. A clinic is one: deciding that something is an ambulance
# case rather than a call to the clinician who performed the procedure is a
# triage judgement, and triage is the one thing a clinic receptionist may never
# make — its own doctor decides. The default wording is right for a short-let
# guest smelling gas and wrong for a patient, which is why it is per tenant.
#
# Two rules for whatever you write here, both load-bearing:
#   1. Keep the promise that a person is being alerted now. A reply naming no
#      number and promising no person is the only genuinely unsafe output.
#   2. For a clinic, name no ambulance or public emergency service. That is the
#      triage judgement the clinic reserves to its own clinician.
#
# Clinic shape (bilingual, Arabic first, {contact} in both paragraphs):
#   دي حالة طارئة ومحتاجة تدخل طبي فوري. برجاء الاتصال حالاً بالطبيب المسؤول على {contact}. أنا بابلغه دلوقتي.
#   \n\nThis is an emergency and needs immediate medical attention. Please call
#   our doctor now on {contact}. I am alerting them right now.
#
# This is clinical safety copy: have the client's medical lead approve the exact
# wording before a real patient can reach the number.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TENANT_URGENT_CONTACT=
TENANT_EMERGENCY_REPLY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The receptionist's own words
# The two ends of a conversation, in the client's voice. Configuration rather
# than code because each names the client, its receptionist and its brand tone
# — the hardcoding tests/test_no_client_name.py exists to prevent. The values
# below are neutral examples: set the client's real Arabic copy in the
# environment, not here. Unset, the tools answer in neutral English.
#
# TENANT_GREETING_OPENING_NAMED may contain {customer_name}. It is a separate
# whole sentence rather than the plain opening with a name appended, because in
# Arabic the name does not sit where an English "Hello X!" would put it.
#
# TENANT_CLOSING_BOOKING_CONFIRMED is the one that can lie: it states an
# appointment exists. It must contain {booking_reference} and is rendered ONLY
# when the scheduling system returned one — otherwise the generic closing is
# sent instead. Do not reword it into something that claims a booking without
# naming its reference.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TENANT_GREETING_OPENING=
TENANT_GREETING_OPENING_NAMED=
TENANT_CLOSING=
TENANT_CLOSING_BOOKING_CONFIRMED=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LLM classifier model IDs — tiering (§8 / D8-a) — NEEDS INPUT
# Pin exact model IDs here, not in code. First pass runs on the cheap tier; the
# message+history re-run on the escalation model when confidence.overall < threshold.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<ANTHROPIC_API_KEY>
OPENAI_API_KEY=<OPENAI_API_KEY>

# Endpoints. Defaults are the public APIs; override to route through a gateway
# inside your own perimeter (the regulated tier forbids unreviewed egress).
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1

CLASSIFIER_MODEL_FIRST_PASS=claude-haiku-4-5            # cheap/fast tier (D8-a: Haiku 4.5)
CLASSIFIER_MODEL_ESCALATION=claude-sonnet-5             # escalation on low confidence (Sonnet 5)
CLASSIFIER_MODEL_FALLBACK=gpt-4o-mini                   # cross-provider peer fallback (OpenAI)
CLASSIFIER_CONFIDENCE_ESCALATION_THRESHOLD=0.85         # high band; medium band floor is 0.5 (see DECISIONS)

# Re-pinning a tier is a config edit, not a code change: the provider is chosen from the
# model ID, and classifier/factory.py sets each family's thinking parameter and output ceiling
# (Claude 5 thinks unless told not to; Fable/Mythos cannot be told not to; older models don't).

# Self-hosted / regulated tier — vLLM-served Qwen (Phase 5, §8) — NEEDS INPUT
# Set SELF_HOSTED_LLM_MODEL as one of the CLASSIFIER_MODEL_* values above to route
# that tier to your own server; it speaks the OpenAI dialect, so no key is required.
SELF_HOSTED_LLM_BASE_URL=<VLLM_OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_URL>
SELF_HOSTED_LLM_MODEL=<QWEN_MODEL_ID>

# Transport limits shared by every LLM provider. The timeout sits in the path of a
# guest waiting for a reply. Retries here are transport-level (429, 5xx, a dropped
# connection); schema-invalid model *output* is retried by the classifier service.
LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
LLM_MAX_RETRIES=2

# Media pipeline ASR / vision — transcription (§6 / D3-a)
ASR_PROVIDER=whisper-api                              # D3-a: Whisper API (SaaS); faster-whisper self-hosted
ASR_MODEL=whisper-1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Database — Postgres (§4). Read by alembic/env.py and by apps/api/db/engine.py.
# Supabase: use the transaction-pooler URI (port 6543) for the app.
# A bare postgresql:// (or legacy postgres://) URI is rewritten onto psycopg 3,
# the driver this project ships, so the URI can be pasted from the dashboard.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DATABASE_URL=<POSTGRES_CONNECTION_URI>

# How the app is connected to Postgres. `transaction` (default) assumes pgbouncer
# in transaction mode — the Supabase 6543 URI — and disables both SQLAlchemy's
# connection pool and psycopg's prepared statements, neither of which survive a
# server connection being reassigned between transactions. Set `session` only for
# a direct connection (5432) or a session-mode pooler, where ordinary pooling is
# safe and faster. Wrong in this direction costs latency; wrong the other way is
# an intermittent "prepared statement already exists" under load.
DATABASE_POOL_MODE=transaction

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Durable queue — arq/Redis (roadmap B5). Read by main.py (the producer) and by
# apps/api/worker.py (the consumer — run separately as `arq apps.api.worker.WorkerSettings`).
# Unset by default, and that is a real mode, not a half-configured one: the API stays on the
# in-process queue it has run on since A4. Set this once both the API and a worker process
# using it exist, or messages will be persisted and never classified.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
REDIS_URL=<REDIS_CONNECTION_URL>
